<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amnisos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amnisos is an ancient harbour site on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a>, that served as a port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>. Excavations revealed a Minoan villa with frescoes of lilies, and the nearby cave of Eileithyia, goddess of childbirth, is mentioned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>. Its name appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets from Knossos as a-mi-ni-so.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amnisos.jpg" alt="Amnisos" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gleb Simonov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heraklion_-_Amnissos_-_Lily_fresco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Amnisos lies 7 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a>, on a beach now used by the people of the modern city. The sea stands three metres higher than in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, and the walls of submerged houses can be seen from the shore. The settlement shares its Bronze Age name with the river that reaches the sea there, now the Karteros, which rises on Mount Ida and runs through the Karteros Ravine; a small island called Amnisos lies off its mouth. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a> made the river's nymphs, the Amnisiades, companions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artemis">Artemis</a>. The road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> was lined with ancient cult sites, among them the cave of Eileithyia, which held objects as old as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> mentions a temple to the goddess there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Spyridon Marinatos excavated the site in 1932 and found a two-storey villa of ten rooms, with a paved court, a hall with a polythyra, a kitchen, a shrine and a bathroom. Its restored fresco, 1.8 metres high, shows red and white lilies, mint, iris and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> in pots, and gave the building its name, the House of the Lilies. It belongs to the naturalistic style that flourished around 1570 to 1470 BCE. The house was later destroyed by fire.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a> records a jar of honey from or at Amnisos for Eleuthia, the goddess of childbirth, probably the one worshipped at the cave. The word a-mi-ni-so was the key to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ventris">Michael Ventris</a>'s decipherment of Linear B: his guess that it named the port of Knossos proved correct and let the rest of the system fall into place. Amnisos also appears on the statue base of Amenophis III at Kom el-Heitan, as a stop of an embassy to Keftiu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, around 1380 BCE. By then the people of Knossos, and almost certainly of its port, spoke Greek. The name's -ssos ending is pre-Greek, so the Mycenaean Greeks probably took it over as they found it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amnisos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the Archaic period its inhabitants spoke the Doric dialect common to most of Crete, and Knossos had become a polis with its own territory, institutions and port at <b>Amnisos</b> and later at Herakleion, the site of modern Heraklion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/knossos">Knossos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Linear B tablets written at Knossos after Greek speakers took over record offerings to a “Potnia of the labyrinth” and to Eleuthia at <b>Amnisos</b>, names that may preserve older Cretan cults.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
